::STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
In a bar chart of favorite apps, the vertical axis is labeled Number of Students. What do the bar heights represent?
{
=A. Counts of students in each app category#The axis label shows that each bar height is a frequency count.
~B. Average screen time for each app#The axis says number of students, not time.
~C. The order in which apps were listed#Bar height represents quantity, not list order.
~D. A relationship between two numerical variables#A bar chart of categories does not show paired numerical data.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A histogram has intervals 0-10, 11-20, and 21-30 minutes. What do the intervals represent?
{
~A. Separate category names#The intervals are numerical ranges, not category labels.
~B. Individual students#Each interval may contain several students.
=C. Ranges of a numerical variable#Histogram intervals group numerical values into ranges.
~D. Percentages that must total 100#Intervals are bins; the bar heights may be counts or relative frequencies depending on the label.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A scatterplot shows hours practiced and quiz score. What should each point represent?
{
=A. One person or case with two numerical values#Each point is a pair of values from the same case.
~B. One category and its count#That describes a bar chart.
~C. A percent of a whole#That describes a pie chart or relative frequency display.
~D. A five-number summary#That describes a box plot.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A graph shows an upward pattern between study time and score. What conclusion is reasonable?
{
~A. Study time definitely causes higher scores#A display alone does not prove cause.
=B. Higher study time is associated with higher scores in the data shown#The display supports an association, not automatic causation.
~C. Scores are categorical data#Scores are numerical.
~D. The graph shows only one variable#A scatterplot pattern uses two variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A pie chart shows 35% bus, 45% car, and 20% walk. What does 45% mean?
{
~A. 45 students always use cars#A percent is not a count unless the total is known.
~B. Cars are 45 units faster than buses#The chart shows category share, not speed.
~C. Car is the median category#Categories do not have a median in a pie chart.
=D. 45% of the group uses car transportation#The slice represents that category share of the whole.
}
